# Certificates — API.md §Certificates
#
# The CA signs the leaves an intercepted HTTPS connection is served with
# (Phase 3); the API-server pair is what the dashboard itself is reached over.
# Import is PEM-only — PKCS#12/PFX is not offered (ADR-0006). Every response
# in this namespace carries Cache-Control: no-store, and no export path can
# reach a private key (SECURITY.md).

@base = {{host}}

### Status: is there a CA, what is its fingerprint and validity window, where
### does the API-server certificate come from, and how is the leaf cache doing.
GET {{base}}/api/v1/certificates
Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}

### Generate (or replace) the certificate authority. `confirm: true` is
### required — a replacement invalidates every client that already trusts the
### current root. The previous pair is copied to /config/ca-archive/<stamp>/,
### never deleted, and archived_previous says whether that happened.
POST {{base}}/api/v1/certificates/ca/generate
Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "confirm": true,
  "common_name": "FastAdHunter CA",
  "validity_days": 3650
}

### Export the CA certificate as PEM (default). Public certificate only.
GET {{base}}/api/v1/certificates/ca/export?format=pem
Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}

### Export as DER — application/pkix-cert, which is what Android expects from
### a .crt download when installing a trusted root.
GET {{base}}/api/v1/certificates/ca/export?format=der
Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}

### Replace the API-server certificate with your own PEM pair. The pair is
### validated (window, key match) and written to /config; the running listener
### keeps the old one, so the answer is restart_required: true.
POST {{base}}/api/v1/certificates/import
Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "format": "pem",
  "cert_pem": "-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\n...\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----\n",
  "key_pem": "-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"
}
